#lang saffron
;; saffron-lang-plan S6 (G7), the half that is easy to get wrong: a literal
;; whose elements HAPPEN to agree is still a `(Vec any)`, not a `(Vec int)`.
;;
;; The widen is unconditional per Saffron file rather than "only when the
;; elements disagree", and that is deliberate.  A vector's element type is not
;; fixed in a dynamic dialect: pushing a string into `[1 2 3]` later is ordinary
;; code, and it is exactly what this fixture does.  Inferring `(Vec int)` from
;; the literal would reject that push, with a diagnostic pointing at the push
;; rather than at the literal that decided it -- a surprise no diagnostic
;; covers.  The cost is one box per element on a homogeneous literal.
;;
;; requires.leak-check: the element boxes are owned by the vector and released
;; with it, including the one pushed after construction.

(defn main [] : int
  (let [v [1 2 3]]
    (println (type-of (vec-get v 0)))
    (vec-push! v (:: "late" any))
    (println (vec-len v))
    (println (type-of (vec-get v 3)))
    (vec-free v))
  0)
